+1, hitting exactly this. We use OpenCode as a headless coding agent in sandboxes. Our auto-sync plugin commits + pushes on `session.idle`, then polls GitLab CI to check if DB migrations passed. Problem is `session.idle` fires after the agent already responded, so the CI result arrives as a delayed follow-up — the user sees "done!" and then a second message about migration status.

We dug into the code and the root cause is straightforward: `hook["event"]?.()` in `plugin/index.ts` L138 isn't awaited. The type signature already says `Promise<void>` but the promise is dropped.

One minimal approach that wouldn't break existing plugins: add an `event.sync` hook alongside `event`. Same signature, but awaited. Plugins that need blocking behavior opt into it, everyone else stays on fire-and-forget `event`.

```typescript
// plugin/index.ts init()
Bus.subscribeAll(async (input) => {
  const hooks = await state().then((x) => x.hooks)
  for (const hook of hooks) {
    hook["event"]?.({ event: input })          // existing — fire-and-forget
    await hook["event.sync"]?.({ event: input }) // new — awaited
  }
})
```

